      "preview_tail": "verall survival (OS) and disease-free survival (DFS). For instance, a higher NLR or PLR has been linked to more aggressive tumor behavior and shorter survival times in patients with high-grade gliomas",
      "full_text": "identifying the glioma grade and molecular subtype is very helpful. However, the traditional way to identify the glioma grade and molecular subtype is to acquire tumor tissue by surgery or biopsy, which causes significant trauma to the human body. The disadvantages of conventional invasive methods motivate us to develop a reliable method for predicting glioma grade and survival. Recently, mounting evidence has suggested that preoperative hematological markers related to nutrition, coagulation, and inflammation are predictive and prognostic factors of cancers (Perisanidis et al., [@B16]; He J. et al., [@B5]; Hwang et al., [@B7]; Ye et al., [@B24]; Zhao et al., [@B26]). In gliomas, neutrophil-to-lymphocyte ratio (NLR) (Han et al., [@B4]; Lopes et al., [@B10]), platelet-to-lymphocyte ratio (PLR) (Han et al., [@B4](Li et al., [@B9]), and lymphocyte-to-monocyte ratio (LMR) (Wang et al., [@B22]) have been identified as potential biomarkers for predicting tumor grade, prognosis, and response to therapy. These hematological markers are derived from routine blood tests, making them minimally invasive options compared to surgical biopsies. The NLR is calculated by dividing the absolute neutrophil count by the absolute lymphocyte count in peripheral blood. An elevated NLR has been associated with a poor prognosis in various cancers, including gliomas (Han et al., [@B4]). Similarly, PLR and LMR are derived from platelet and monocyte counts relative to lymphocytes, respectively. These ratios reflect systemic inflammation and immune response status, which can influence tumor progression and patient outcomes. Recent studies have shown that these hematological markers not only correlate with glioma grade but also provide valuable prognostic information regarding overall survival (OS) and disease-free survival (DFS). For instance, a higher NLR or PLR has been linked to more aggressive tumor behavior and shorter survival times in patients with high-grade gliomas",
