      "preview_head": "farms with known outbreaks of the disease and testing for the presence of L. intracellularis using Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR). The farms were located in different regions, and the gilts were test",
      "preview_tail": "ough the sale and movement of replacement gilts. These measures are essential for maintaining the health and productivity of breeding herds and for minimizing the economic losses associated with proli",
      "full_text": "farms with known outbreaks of the disease and testing for the presence of L. intracellularis using Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR). The farms were located in different regions, and the gilts were tested at three time points: before purchase, two weeks post-purchase, and at the end of their reproductive cycle (about 6 months post-purchase). The results showed that L. intracellularis was detected in a significant number of gilts at all three time points, indicating that the pathogen could be transmitted through the sale and movement of gilts. This suggests that biosecurity measures, such as isolation and testing of new arrivals, are crucial in preventing the spread of the disease within herds. In addition, the study also evaluated the impact of L. intracellularis infection on the growth performance of the gilts. It was observed that gilts infected with L. intracellularis had a lower average daily gain and a higher feed conversion ratio compared to gilts that remained negative for the infection. This highlights the importance of managing and controlling L. intracellularis to ensure optimal growth and efficiency of breeding herds. Overall, this study emphasizes the need for vigilant monitoring and stringent biosecurity practices to mitigate the spread of L. intracellularis in pig herds, particularly through the sale and movement of replacement gilts. These measures are essential for maintaining the health and productivity of breeding herds and for minimizing the economic losses associated with proli",
